increasing numbers of Europeans of  crossing borders like this one into  Syria to fight alongside the opposition  Free Syrian Army many are idealists but  some of these foreign fighters return  radicalized by their experience and  compose a danger to the nations of the  EU it's a phenomenon that interior  ministers will discuss this week in  luxembourg based on a document prepared  by the EU counterterrorism coordinator  you need to work on on how to prevent  people from leaving europe to go to  syria and what you do when they get back  you need to mobilize the classic of  response more information sharing among  the intelligence services and the law  enforcement agencies get a better sense  of of special travels and that requires  that you collect more travel data and  finally we need to engage more with the  country of transit the country from from  where a lot of foreign fighters are  coming from so get a bit more  international cooperation on that front  the problem of people crossing Europe's  borders to fight and train in Syria but  also places like Somalia or Yemen has  taken on greater prominence since the  killing of a British soldier in London  and the stabbing of a French soldier  last month British and French ministers  will update their colleagues on those  investigations more broadly the  ministers will discuss ways to improve  their analysis of the problem prevent  people from wanting to leave on such  missions pursue those who do by using  travel data and imposing legal or  administrative sanctions and look to  boosting cooperation with international  partners in separate discussions the  council will look at ways to improve the  protection of refugees fleeing Syria to  places like Jordan Lebanon and Turkey  more than four million people have been  forced from their homes during the  two-year long conflict
 

